<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coup of 18 Brumaire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Coup of 18 Brumaire was the seizure of power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> on 9–10 November 1799 (18–19 Brumaire, Year VIII, in the revolutionary calendar). With the help of the director Emmanuel-Joseph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Sieyès</a> and of troops who dispersed the legislative councils, Bonaparte overthrew the Directory and set up the Consulate, with himself as First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a>. It is usually taken as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coup-of-18-brumaire.jpg" alt="Coup of 18 Brumaire" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François Bouchot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bouchot_-_Le_general_Bonaparte_au_Conseil_des_Cinq-Cents.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The French Republic under the Directory had been at war with the major European powers since 1792. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès</a>, elected to the Directory in May 1799, opposed the Constitution of 1795, which gave the Directory limited powers and no parliamentary control, and saw a coup as the only way to change it. He looked for a strong man who would restore the Republic on his terms and first had General Joubert in mind; when Joubert fell at the Battle of Novi in August 1799, he turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> on the advice of General Moreau.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 16 October but was under the minimum age of 40 for the Directory. When Paul Barras refused to support a coup on 4 November, Napoleon allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Sieyès</a>. The coup was planned for 7 November and postponed by 24 hours to Saturday, 9 November.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 9 November the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-elders-carthage">Council of Elders</a> was summoned to the Tuileries, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Sieyès</a> persuaded it, on the pretext that a Jacobin plot was imminent, to move the legislature to the Château de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cloud">Saint-Cloud</a>, outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> command of the troops in the Paris area. The directors Gohier and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-moulin">Jean Moulin</a> were placed under house arrest, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand</a> persuaded Barras to resign. On 10 November Napoleon had the palace at Saint-Cloud surrounded by 6,000 soldiers.</p><p>In the Orangery, where the Council of Five Hundred sat, deputies shouted 'Kill him!' and tried to seize him, and Murat and Lefebvre saved him, dazed and bleeding. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucien-bonaparte">Lucien Bonaparte</a> told the guards that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> had been attacked, and the chamber was cleared by force. By 2 am Lucien had gathered a quorum of stragglers from the Five Hundred and fifty members of the Elders, who dissolved the Directory and swore allegiance to three provisional consuls: Napoleon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Sieyès</a> and Ducos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> moved to isolate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Sieyès</a>, who resigned after Napoleon publicly criticised his ideas. Napoleon kept the main features of his draft, with four assemblies able to block one another and ministers answering only to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a>. The constitution was adopted on 12 December, with Jean-Jacques Cambacérès and Charles Lebrun as co-consuls.</p><p>It was put to a referendum, which gave 3,011,007 votes for and 1,562 against. Half a million of these votes were cast by the army without valid ballots, and some 900,000 were forged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucien-bonaparte">Lucien Bonaparte</a>. With a turnout of 25 per cent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> secured only about 17 per cent of the genuine votes, on this reckoning. Most historians regard the coup as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Coup of 18 Brumaire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
